// `Param` joins the `@attribute(...)` placement vocabulary, so an attribute can declare itself
// parameter-only — and, symmetrically, an attribute restricted to some OTHER kind is rejected on a
// parameter. `@attribute(Field)` names a struct/class field; a parameter is a different site, so
// writing `#[Column]` on one is a placement error (E0030) at the misplaced attribute, exactly as it
// would be on a method. The parameter is a target kind like any other, not a hole in the rule.
// expect: error E0030 at 11:11
// expect: exit 1
@attribute(Field)
struct Column { name: string }

fn save(#[Column("id")] id: int): void { return; }
